On 5 Jul., 08:56, "John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Jul 4, 5:38 am, Henry J Cobb <h...@io.com> wrote:
>
> > Far too many items become almost useless once you have one unicorn horn.
>
> I think you are exactly right on this.  (And I've said roughly this
> same thing before, but no matter.)

I can't say that I'd disagree about the powers of a unicorn horn but I
see it in a different light.

> There are a huge number of things in the game that become essentially
> no problem once the player gains a unicorn horn.  It cures most bad
> states quickly enough to matter in more than 99% of cases (based on
> personal experience)

The non-reliability in critical situations had been a problem more
than once in my games. (It has been often mentioned that 0% failure
in cure sickness is a lot better; just to mention one of the most
critical.)

The most powerful property is indeed restoring of all stats. I cannot
quite see why Str, Dex, Con, Int, Wis, Cha should be restored. Plus
hallucination, stunning, confusion, blindness, etc.; there are also
other means to cure the latter (like sickness, lizard, carrot, etc.)
and there's a potion and a spell for the former.

> AND it well-enough identifies multiple potion types

At the point when you meet (and can kill) unicorns identification of
those few potions is not that important any more. And you don't even
need to identify the potions by dipping; it's easier to quaff them and
immediately use the horn or other means to cure you, if identification
matters.

> AND is a source of water for blessing

Diluting potions in fountains and pools or moats is much easier at
that point in the game and it is not restricted to the few potions
which can be cleared by the horn (even the wand of cancellation is
superior). Furthermore you might not want to dilute confusion; these
potions are too helpful.

> AND is a well-above-average weapon

Depending on whether you can use it at all. Off-top my head I cannot
remember any character that wouldn't have had a better choice in his
games than using a unicorn horn. And it's two-handed. And you need to
increase your skill and waste slots.

> AND is simple enough to obtain because all dead unicorns leave one.

You need sufficient speed (maybe displacement or invisibility) and be
not too wimpy to be able to corner it before you kill it. And that the
horn is a guaranteed "death drop" is just consistent.

I think the time when unicorns appear is well designed and the danger
involved and difficulty in getting the horn (surely not equally
difficult for all classes and all game constellations) well balanced.

> This is simply overpowered.  I have never seen
> anyone come up with a good counter to this argument.

Yes, the unicorn horn comprises quite some powers. And restoring lost
stats is IMO the most powerful property of the unihorn. But for most
other properties there are other (often much better) solutions.

But having a unicorn support a restricted feature adds to the options
a player has in the game. Frankly, I fear that if it's too specialized
and there'd be always very few (or just one!) solution to any problem
it would take away a lot from Nethack.

Characters suffer so much in the early game that finding and killing a
unicorn is like a quest (for me). And you are not even guaranteed to
find one early enough as you'd need it, and when you got one you have
also a couple other means to solve your issues.

Should it's properties be specialized further? Say, remove the ability
to restore the stats? Maybe - potions of restore ability would become
of some use then. But I don't think that it's necessary to remove all
its less significant properties where there are much better choices.

One could also implement some latency; the unicorn horn would restore
one (or a few) points of lost stats and be ineffective for a couple
(hundreds) of turns. But I am also fine with it as it is designed now.

> I think where we differ is in how its power should be reduced.  I
> think making it degrade with non-weapon use is enough, is the least-
> intrusive way to do it.  In fact, if I have my facts right, Nethack+
> did just that.  They are bulky enough that carrying around more than
> one would be a better counter to its effects.  It might not be enough,
> but it seems a better idea that making negative enchantment have some
> kind of positive effect.

